s=A.a([A.c9(A.F(A.a([A.uh(A.G(20),A.kB("assets/images/logo.png",B.X_,80,80),B.c6),B.D,A.d(A.c("appName"),o,o,o,o,A.e(o,o,$.a5(),o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,22,o,o,B.z,o,o,!0,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o),o,o,o),A.d("Bharatheeyam \u2014 Vedic Astrology",o,o,o,o,A.e(o,o,$.L(),o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,14,o,o,o,o,o,!0,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o),o,o,o),A.d("Version 1.0.1  \u2022  Open Source (AGPL-3.0)",o,o,o,o,A.e(o,o,$.L(),o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,12,o,o,o,o,o,!0,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o),o,o,o)],l),B.i,B.f,B.h,B.k),o,o),B.ax,A.kj("\u0cb2\u0cc6\u0c95\u0ccd\u0c95\u0cbe\u0c9a\u0cbe\u0cb0 \u0c8e\u0c82\u0c9c\u0cbf\u0ca8\u0ccd / Calculation Engine"),A.p2("All planetary positions are calculated using the Swiss Ephemeris \u2014 the world's most accurate astronomical positional engine, developed by Astrodienst AG (Z\xfcrich, Switzerland). It is accurate to sub-arcsecond precision and covers dates from 5400 BCE to 5400 CE.\n\nThe engine takes: birth date, time (in UTC), latitude, and longitude as input and computes precise sidereal planetary longitudes.",B.a5D,"Swiss Ephemeris (sweph)"),B.bz,A.kj("\u0c85\u0caf\u0ca8\u0cbe\u0c82\u0cb6 / Ayanamsa"),A.p2("The Swiss Ephemeris returns tropical (Western) longitudes. Vedic astrology uses the sidereal zodiac. The difference between tropical and sidereal is called Ayanamsa.\n\n\u2022 Lahiri (Chitrapaksha) \u2014 Official Indian government standard. Most widely used in India.\n\u2022 KP (Krishnamurti Paddhati) \u2014 A slightly different Ayanamsa used in KP astrology system.\n\u2022 Raman \u2014 Developed by B.V. Raman, another popular choice.\n\nSidereal Longitude = Tropical Longitude \u2212 Ayanamsa",B.a5b,"Tropical \u2192 Sidereal Conversion"),B.bz,A.kj("\u0c95\u0cc1\u0c82\u0ca1\u0cb2\u0cbf / Birth Chart"),A.p2("After computing sidereal longitudes, the app places each planet (Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn, Rahu, Ketu, Mandi, and Gulika) into its Rashi (sign) and Nakshatra (lunar mansion).\n\n\ud83d\udcd0 House Cusps: Calculated using the Whole Sign house system \u2014 each house spans exactly one zodiac sign starting from the Lagna (Ascendant).\n\n\ud83c\udf19 Rahu & Ketu (Lunar Nodes): The app supports both True Node (actual astronomical position) and Mean Node (average position). Rahu and Ketu are always 180\xb0 apart.\n\n\u26a1 Mandi & Gulika: Calculated from the day's sunrise/sunset times and the weekday lord sequence using the classical Parashari method.",B.a5y,"Graha Sphutas & House Cusps"),B.bz,A.kj("\u0caa\u0c82\u0c9a\u0cbe\u0c82\u0c97 / Panchanga"),A.p2("The Panchanga has five elements, each calculated from the Sun and Moon's positions:\n\n1. \u0cb5\u0cbe\u0cb0 (Vara) \u2014 Day of the week determined by the lord of the hour at sunrise. Each day has a ruling planet.\n\n2. \u0ca4\u0cbf\u0ca5\u0cbf (Tithi) \u2014 Lunar day. Calculated as the angular distance between Sun and Moon divided by 12\xb0. Each 12\xb0 = 1 Tithi. There are 30 Tithis in a lunar month.\n\n3. \u0ca8\u0c95\u0ccd\u0cb7\u0ca4\u0ccd\u0cb0 (Nakshatra) \u2014 Lunar mansion. The ecliptic is divided into 27 equal segments of 13\xb020' each. The Moon's position determines the current Nakshatra.\n\n4. \u0caf\u0ccb\u0c97 (Yoga) \u2014 Calculated by adding the sidereal longitudes of Sun + Moon, then dividing by 13\xb020'. There are 27 Yogas.\n\n5. \u0c95\u0cb0\u0ca3 (Karana) \u2014 Half a Tithi (6\xb0). There are 11 types of Karanas \u2014 4 fixed and 7 repeating.\n\n\u2600\ufe0f Sunrise & Sunset are calculated using the Swiss Ephemeris rise/set algorithm for the exact birth coordinates.",B.a5x,"Five Limbs of the Hindu Calendar"),B.bz,A.kj("\u0caa\u0c82\u0c9a\u0cbe\u0c82\u0c97 \u0cb9\u0cac\u0ccd\u0cac\u0c97\u0cb3 \u0c86\u0ca7\u0cbe\u0cb0 / Panchanga Events Reference")],l)
B.d.G(s,A.bMo())
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0cb5\u0cbf\u0c82\u0cb6\u0ccb\u0ca4\u0ccd\u0ca4\u0cb0\u0cbf \u0ca6\u0cb6 / Vimshottari Dasha"))
s.push(A.p2("Vimshottari Dasha is a 120-year planetary period system based on the Moon's Nakshatra at birth.\n\n\ud83d\udd11 Start Point: The balance of the Dasha at birth is calculated from how far the Moon has traversed its birth Nakshatra.\n\nBalance = (1 \u2212 nakshatra_traversed_fraction) \xd7 lord's_total_years\n\nDasha Lords and their periods (total 120 years):\nKetu 7 \u2022 Venus 20 \u2022 Sun 6 \u2022 Moon 10 \u2022 Mars 7 \u2022 Rahu 18 \u2022 Jupiter 16 \u2022 Saturn 19 \u2022 Mercury 17\n\n\ud83d\udcca Antardasha (sub-periods) are calculated by proportional subdivision: each Mahadasha is split into 9 Antardashas in the same lord sequence, proportional to period lengths.",B.a5H,"Planetary Period System"))
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0cb7\u0ca1\u0ccd\u0cac\u0cb2 / Shadbala"))
s.push(A.p2("Shadbala measures the total strength of each planet in 6 categories, expressed in Rupas and Shashtiamshas:\n\n1. Sthana Bala (\u0cb8\u0ccd\u0ca5\u0cbe\u0ca8 \u0cac\u0cb2) \u2014 Positional strength: Uccha (exaltation), Saptavargaja (divisional chart placement), Ojayugmarashyamsha, Kendradi, and Drekkana strengths.\n\n2. Dig Bala (\u0ca6\u0cbf\u0c97\u0ccd \u0cac\u0cb2) \u2014 Directional strength. Each planet is strongest in a specific house quadrant.\n\n3. Kala Bala (\u0c95\u0cbe\u0cb2 \u0cac\u0cb2) \u2014 Temporal strength: Natonnata (day/night), Paksha (lunar fortnight), Tribhaga, Abda, Masa, Vara, Hora, Ayana, and Yuddha (planetary war).\n\n4. Chesta Bala (\u0c9a\u0cc7\u0cb7\u0ccd\u0c9f \u0cac\u0cb2) \u2014 Motional strength based on retrograde/direct motion and speed.\n\n5. Naisargika Bala (\u0ca8\u0cc8\u0cb8\u0cb0\u0ccd\u0c97\u0cbf\u0c95 \u0cac\u0cb2) \u2014 Natural/inherent strength: Fixed order: Saturn < Mars < Mercury < Jupiter < Venus < Moon < Sun.\n\n6. Drik Bala (\u0ca6\u0cc3\u0c95\u0ccd \u0cac\u0cb2) \u2014 Aspectual strength from other planets.\n\nMinimum required Rupas benchmarks are used to evaluate whether each planet meets its strength threshold.",B.a4v,"6-Fold Planetary Strength"))
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0cb5\u0cb0\u0ccd\u0c97 \u0c9a\u0c95\u0ccd\u0cb0\u0c97\u0cb3\u0cc1 / Divisional Charts"))
s.push(A.p2("Each planet's sign is subdivided into smaller equal segments:\n\n\u2022 D-1 (Rashi/Lagna chart) \u2014 Full 30\xb0 of each sign. The primary chart.\n\n\u2022 D-9 (Navamsha) \u2014 Each sign is divided into 9 equal parts of 3\xb020'. The Navamsha describes the inner nature of the planet and is crucial for assessing marriage and dharma.\n\n\u2022 D-12 (Dwadashamsha) \u2014 Each sign is divided into 12 equal parts of 2\xb030'. Used to assess parents and ancestral karma.\n\n\u2022 D9 of D9 \u2014 The Navamsha within the Navamsha, giving a deeper sub-divisional reading.",B.a5B,"D-1, D-9, D-12 Subdivisions"))
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0c86\u0cb0\u0cc2\u0ca2 \u0cb2\u0c97\u0ccd\u0ca8 / Arudha Lagna"))
s.push(A.p2("The Arudha Lagna represents how the world perceives you \u2014 the image or Maya you project.\n\n\ud83d\udcd0 Calculation:\n1. Count houses from Lagna to its lord.\n2. Count the same number of houses from the lord.\n3. The resulting house is the Arudha Lagna.\n\nException: If the result falls in the 1st or 7th house from Lagna, move it 10 houses forward (classical Parashari rule).\n\nThe app calculates Arudhas for all 12 Bhavas (A1\u2013A12) using this same method applied to each house lord.",B.a5t,"Maya & Manifestation"))
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0ca4\u0cbe\u0cb0\u0cbe\u0ca8\u0cc1\u0c95\u0cc2\u0cb2 / Taranukula"))
s.push(A.p2("Taranukula evaluates the 27 Nakshatras relative to the Janma (birth) Nakshatra using a 9-fold cycle:\n\n1. Janma (Birth) \u2014 The Nakshatra itself.\n2. Sampat (Wealth)\n3. Vipat (Danger/Loss)\n4. Kshema (Well-being)\n5. Pratyak (Obstacle)\n6. Sadhana (Achievement)\n7. Naidhana (Death/End)\n8. Mitra (Friend)\n9. Parama Mitra (Best Friend)\n\nThe cycle repeats every 9 Nakshatras. Beneficial: Sampat, Kshema, Sadhana, Mitra, Parama Mitra.\nInauspicious: Vipat, Pratyak, Naidhana.",B.a5G,"Star Compatibility from Birth Nakshatra"))
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0cae\u0cc7\u0cb3\u0cbe\u0caa\u0c95 / Melapaka (Compatibility)"))
s.push(A.p2("Melapaka assesses marriage compatibility by comparing the Rashi and Nakshatra of two people across 8 Kootas:\n\n1. Varna (1 pt) \u2014 Spiritual compatibility by caste category.\n2. Vashya (2 pt) \u2014 Control/dominance relationship between signs.\n3. Tara (3 pt) \u2014 Star compatibility using the Taranukula cycle.\n4. Yoni (4 pt) \u2014 Sexual/physical compatibility by animal symbols.\n5. Graha Maitri (5 pt) \u2014 Friendship between Moon sign lords.\n6. Gana (6 pt) \u2014 Nature: Deva (divine), Manushya (human), Rakshasa (demon).\n7. Rashi (7 pt) \u2014 Sign compatibility (Bhakuta): positive/negative relationships between Rashis.\n8. Nadi (8 pt) \u2014 Energy channel: Vata, Pitta, Kapha. Same Nadi = 0 points (Nadi Dosha).\n\nMaximum = 36 points. Score \u2265 18 is considered compatible.",B.a4M,"36-Point Marriage Compatibility"))
s.push(B.bz)
s.push(A.kj("\u0c89\u0caa\u0c97\u0ccd\u0cb0\u0cb9\u0c97\u0cb3\u0cc1 / Upagrahas & Special Points"))
s.push(A.p2("\ud83d\udd34 Mandi & Gulika: Calculated from sunrise/sunset times and the hora (planetary hour) sequence for the day. Mandi is the son of Saturn and is considered inauspicious.\n\n\u2b50 Advanced Sphutas (16 points) include:\n\u2022 Bhava Lagna, Hora Lagna, Ghati Lagna \u2014 time-based Lagnas.\n\u2022 Pushkara Navamsha & Bhaga \u2014 auspicious sub-divisions.\n\u2022 Shree Lagna, Indu Lagna, Pranapada \u2014 derived special ascendants.\n\u2022 Varnada Lagna \u2014 used for Varna calculation in Jaimini.\n\u2022 Yogi, Avayogi, Duplicate Yogi \u2014 fortune/misfortune points calculated from Sun + Moon longitude + 93\xb020'.",B.a5s,"Mandi, Gulika & 16 Sphutas"))
s.push(B.ax)
s.push(A.dR($.ao(),o,o))
s.push(B.a9)
